%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level1_TheFirstCase
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_1
  levelNumber: 1
  displayName: The First Case
  sceneName: Game
  description: "Your first day on the case. Texts and emails arrive while you explore
    the house \u2014 decide which are scams."
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3c2461ec3d7cb3f4195d1800bbe3a169, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: CASE BRIEFING
  briefingText: Welcome to your first case. You will receive messages and emails
    throughout the day. Some are legitimate, while others may be attempts to deceive
    you. Examine each message carefully before deciding.
  caseNotes:
  - Earlier today you ordered a desk lamp through the official Example Delivery app.
  - During this morning's setup, you requested a password change for your Example
    Cloud Service account.
  objectives:
  - Identify five messages.
  - Correctly classify each message as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
  - Explore the house while waiting for incoming messages.
  messages:
  - id: l1_package_hold
    channel: 0
    sender: Unknown Number
    senderDetail: +1 (555) 014-2290
    subject: 
    body: 'Delivery Alert: Your package is on hold due to incomplete delivery information.
      Please confirm your details using the link provided.'
    link: http://parcel-redelivery.example/confirm
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The sender is unknown, and the message creates urgency while asking
      you to use an unfamiliar link. Verify delivery information through the courier's
      official website or app instead.
    trigger: 1
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sender
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Sender name
      finding: Shown only as 'Unknown Number' - it isn't in your contacts and doesn't
        name any delivery company.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: unknown
    - id: number
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Phone number
      finding: +1 (555) 014-2290 is an ordinary mobile number. Real couriers normally
        text from a named sender or short code.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: number
    - id: heading
      field: 3
      match: Delivery Alert
      label: Heading
      finding: '''Delivery Alert'' sounds official - but anyone can type a heading.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: heading
    - id: hold
      field: 3
      match: Your package is on hold
      label: The claim
      finding: It never says which package, which company or which order.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: vague
    - id: details
      field: 3
      match: confirm your details
      label: What it asks
      finding: It wants you to 'confirm your details' through a text link. Couriers
        don't need you to re-enter personal details this way.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: details
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Link
      finding: parcel-redelivery.example is not a courier you use, and the link is
        plain http (not secure).
      linkAddress: http://parcel-redelivery.example/confirm
      clueId: link
    evidence:
    - id: unknown
      title: Unknown sender
      whyItMatters: The text doesn't come from any company you recognise.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: number
      title: Ordinary mobile number
      whyItMatters: Delivery companies don't usually text from random mobile numbers.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: vague
      title: No order or company named
      whyItMatters: A real delivery update names the company and your parcel.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: details
      title: Asks you to confirm personal details
      whyItMatters: Scam texts collect personal and payment details through links.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: link
      title: Unfamiliar, insecure link
      whyItMatters: parcel-redelivery.example isn't a courier you use, and it's plain
        http.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: heading
      title: Official-sounding heading
      whyItMatters: Anyone can write 'Delivery Alert' - on its own it isn't evidence.
      supportsVerdict: 0
  - id: l1_bank_verify
    channel: 1
    sender: "Security Team \u2014 Example Bank"
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Immediate Account Verification Required
    body: We detected an issue with your account. Your access may be restricted today
      unless you verify your information using the link below.
    link: http://examplebank-verify.example/login
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The message pressures you to act quickly and requests account verification
      through a link. Do not provide sensitive information through unexpected messages.
      Contact the bank through its official channels.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sender
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Sender
      finding: The display name claims to be Example Bank's security team, but no
        address is shown to back it up - names are easy to fake.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
    - id: subject
      field: 2
      match: Immediate
      label: Subject line
      finding: '''Immediate'' action required: pressure to act before you think.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: urgent
    - id: issue
      field: 3
      match: We detected an issue with your account
      label: The problem
      finding: It never says what the issue is or which account.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: vague
    - id: threat
      field: 3
      match: Your access may be restricted today
      label: Threat
      finding: Threatens to restrict your access today unless you act.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: threat
    - id: verify
      field: 3
      match: verify your information
      label: What it asks
      finding: Asks you to verify your information through a link. Banks tell you
        to sign in through their app or official site yourself.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: request
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Link
      finding: examplebank-verify.example is not Example Bank's own site - it's a
        separate look-alike domain built to collect logins.
      linkAddress: http://examplebank-verify.example/login
      clueId: link
    evidence:
    - id: urgent
      title: '''Immediate'' action required'
      whyItMatters: Urgency stops you from checking first.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: threat
      title: Threat to restrict your account today
      whyItMatters: Scammers use threats to rush you.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: vague
      title: Vague 'issue' with no details
      whyItMatters: Real security emails say what happened.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: request
      title: Asks you to verify information via a link
      whyItMatters: A bank won't ask you to verify details through an email link.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: link
      title: Look-alike bank domain
      whyItMatters: examplebank-verify.example isn't the bank's real site.
      supportsVerdict: 1
  - id: l1_order_confirmed
    channel: 0
    sender: Example Delivery
    senderDetail: 
    subject: 
    body: Your order has been confirmed. Your estimated delivery date is tomorrow.
      You can view your order status in the official delivery app.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The player previously placed an order, so this message matches
      an expected transaction.
    explanation: The message matches an action you took and directs you to the official
      app rather than an unfamiliar link. In real life, still verify the sender and
      order details.
    trigger: 1
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sender
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Sender
      finding: Example Delivery - the delivery service for the order you placed.
        (Names can be faked, so check the rest too.)
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: known
    - id: confirmed
      field: 3
      match: Your order has been confirmed
      label: What it says
      finding: It confirms the order you placed earlier - it matches something you
        actually did.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: matches
    - id: date
      field: 3
      match: Your estimated delivery date is tomorrow
      label: Delivery date
      finding: 'Just information: no deadline, threat or payment.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: noask
    - id: app
      field: 3
      match: view your order status in the official delivery app
      label: Next step
      finding: It sends you to the official app you already use - there's no link
        to click.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: app
    evidence:
    - id: matches
      title: Matches your real order
      whyItMatters: You did place an order, so this message is expected.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: noask
      title: Asks for nothing
      whyItMatters: No payment, details, link or deadline.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: app
      title: Points to the official app
      whyItMatters: Using the app yourself is the safe way to check.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: known
      title: From the delivery service you used
      whyItMatters: Consistent with your order - supporting evidence, not proof on
        its own.
      supportsVerdict: 1
  - id: l1_prize
    channel: 1
    sender: Rewards Center
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Congratulations! You Won a Special Prize!
    body: You have been selected to receive a free premium reward. Claim your prize
      immediately by opening the link below. No purchase is required!
    link: http://rewards-claim.example/prize
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The unexpected reward and pressure to claim it immediately are warning
      signs. Verify promotions independently and avoid unfamiliar claim links.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sender
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Sender
      finding: '''Rewards Center'' - not a company you know, and no address is shown.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: unknown
    - id: subject
      field: 2
      match: You Won a Special Prize!
      label: Subject line
      finding: You 'won' a prize for a contest you never entered.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: neverentered
    - id: free
      field: 3
      match: free premium reward
      label: The offer
      finding: A free premium reward out of nowhere - too good to be true.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: toogood
    - id: now
      field: 3
      match: Claim your prize immediately
      label: Urgency
      finding: Pushes you to claim immediately.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: urgency
    - id: nopurchase
      field: 3
      match: No purchase is required!
      label: Reassurance
      finding: '''No purchase required'' is meant to lower your guard.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: nopurchase
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Link
      finding: rewards-claim.example isn't connected to anything you use. Prize links
        like this lead to pages asking for personal or card details.
      linkAddress: http://rewards-claim.example/prize
      clueId: link
    evidence:
    - id: neverentered
      title: A prize you never entered for
      whyItMatters: You can't win a contest you didn't enter.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: unknown
      title: Unknown sender
      whyItMatters: '''Rewards Center'' isn''t a company you deal with.'
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: toogood
      title: Too good to be true
      whyItMatters: Free premium rewards out of nowhere are classic bait.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: urgency
      title: '''Claim immediately'''
      whyItMatters: Urgency stops you from thinking it through.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: link
      title: Unknown prize-claim link
      whyItMatters: The link isn't connected to any company you use.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: nopurchase
      title: '''No purchase required'''
      whyItMatters: Real promotions say this too - on its own it proves nothing.
      supportsVerdict: 0
  - id: l1_password_done
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Cloud Service
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Your Password Update Is Complete
    body: The password update you requested has been completed. If you made this
      change, no further action is needed. If you did not request it, open the official
      application and review your account security.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: The player previously requested a password change during the
      simulated setup.
    explanation: The notification matches an action you requested and gives a safe
      way to check your account. If you did not make the change, investigate through
      the official application.
    trigger: 2
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sender
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: Sender
      finding: Example Cloud Service - the service you changed your password on.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: service
    - id: subject
      field: 2
      match: 
      label: Subject line
      finding: A calm, factual subject - no urgency or threats.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
    - id: requested
      field: 3
      match: The password update you requested
      label: What it refers to
      finding: It refers to the password change you really made during setup.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: matches
    - id: noaction
      field: 3
      match: no further action is needed
      label: What it asks
      finding: Nothing - no link, code or password is requested.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: noask
    - id: official
      field: 3
      match: open the official application
      label: Advice
      finding: If it wasn't you, it tells you to open the official app yourself -
        not to click a link.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: official
    evidence:
    - id: matches
      title: Matches the change you made
      whyItMatters: You did request a password change, so this notice is expected.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: noask
      title: Asks for nothing
      whyItMatters: No link, code or password is requested.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: official
      title: Tells you to use the official app
      whyItMatters: Genuine services send you to their own app, not a link.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: service
      title: From the service you used
      whyItMatters: Consistent with your change - supporting evidence, not proof
        on its own.
      supportsVerdict: 1
  inboxExtras:
  - id: old_library
    channel: 1
    sender: Riverside Public Library
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Your reserved book is ready for pickup
    body: The book you reserved is now available at the front desk. It will be held
      for 7 days. Log in to your library account if you need more time.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
  - id: old_team
    channel: 1
    sender: Sam (Volunteer Team)
    senderDetail: 
    subject: 'Saturday clean-up: meeting point'
    body: Hi everyone, we'll meet at the park entrance at 9:00 on Saturday. Bring
      gloves and water. See you there!
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
  - id: old_receipt
    channel: 1
    sender: Example Delivery
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Receipt for your desk lamp order
    body: Thank you for your order of 1 x desk lamp. You can track this order in
      the official Example Delivery app.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 
    trigger: 0
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
  caseFiles: []
  firstMessageDelay: 8
  delayBetweenMessages: {x: 10, y: 20}
  minimumActivityDelay: 4
  oneStarScore: 200
  twoStarScore: 325
  threeStarScore: 425
  minScoreToUnlockNext: 325
  scoring:
    basePoints: 100
    speedBonus: 0
    fastWindow: 0.35
    wrongPenalty: 10
    recoveryPoints: 20
    baseReadingSeconds: 25
    secondsPerWord: 0.6
    secondsPerDetail: 4
    minReadingSeconds: 45
    maxReadingSeconds: 150
    caseExtraSeconds: 60
    overtimeStepSeconds: 15
    overtimePenaltyPerStep: 0
    maxTimePenalty: 0
    minCorrectPoints: 100
  nextLevelId: level_2
  nextSceneName: Room2_InvestigationOffice
  lessons:
  - Check who really sent a message. Unknown numbers and vague senders deserve a
    closer look.
  - Urgency and threats ("today", "immediately", "restricted") are pressure tactics.
  - Don't use links in unexpected messages. Open the official app or website yourself.
  - Unexpected prizes and rewards are classic bait.
  - A message that matches something you actually did, and points to the official
    app, is more likely legitimate - but still verify.
  learningSummary: 
  performanceHigh: 
  performanceModerate: 
  performanceLow: 
